    "q-96859": {
      "dom_id": "step-q96859",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "A computer program is designed so that it can randomly generate a real number between $0$ and $10,$ inclusive. What is the probability that the number generated is between $3$ and $7?$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $X$ represent the number generated by the program. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U[0,10],$ and we wish to compute $P(3<X<7).$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a}, & a\\le x\\le b \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U[0,10],$ we have the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}, & 0\\le x\\le 10 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3<X<7) & ={\\int }_{3}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{7}\\frac{1}{10}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{x}{10}|}_{3}^{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{10}-\\frac{3}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-96859-a",
