    "q-96922": {
      "dom_id": "step-q96922",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "The minimum time it takes a plumber to fix a leaky pipe is $30$ minutes, and the maximum time is $110$ minutes.  Assuming that the plumber's time to fix a leaky pipe is uniformly distributed, what is the expected time to fix a pipe?",
      "worked_solution": "Let $X$ represent the time to fix a randomly chosen leaky pipe. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U[30,110],$ and we wish to compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U[30,110],$ we have the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{30+110}{2}=70\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean time to fix the leaky pipe is $70\\,\\text{min}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
