    "q-96961": {
      "dom_id": "step-q96961",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "The duration of a tv program is uniformly distributed between $24$ and $36$ minutes, inclusive. What is the variance of the TV program duration?",
      "worked_solution": "Let $X$ represent the duration of a tv program. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U[24,36],$ and we wish to compute $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{(b-a)^{2}}{12}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U[24,36],$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{(36-24)^{2}}{12}=\\frac{144}{12}=12\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance of the TV program duration is $12\\,{\\text{min}}^{2}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
